  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCold Start to the Week...\nA 1035 mb high is centered over the Dakotas this afternoon, \nwith ridging extending south across the central and southern \nGreat Plains. Northwest flow aloft ahead of the ridge axis will \ncontinue to drive temperatures down locally, with lows bottoming\nout in the low 20s area-wide tonight into Tuesday morning under\nstrong radiational cooling conditions. Temperatures will only \nrebound into the lower to mid-40s Tuesday afternoon under \nincreasing cloud cover ahead of our approaching midweek system.\n\nWintry Mix and Snow (Late Wednesday into Thursday)...\nA pair of strong shortwaves are progged to move across the \ncentral CONUS midweek. A northern stream wave will dig across \nthe Dakotas on Wednesday morning, while a southern stream wave \npushes east across the lower Mississippi Valley. These waves \nwill deepen into a pair of closed upper lows Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning, while at the surface, an inverted trough\npivots across parts of the state.\n\nThis system presents significant forecast challenges and high \nuncertainty regarding precipitation type and snow accumulation, \nparticularly near any mesoscale banding. As the inverted trough \npushes west into east-central Illinois late Wednesday afternoon \nand evening, southeast flow aloft will introduce a warm nose\ninto eastern parts of the state. Combined with surface \ntemperatures above freezing, this will result in initial rain. \nAs the trough axis pushes back east late Wednesday evening \nthrough Thursday morning, colder air filtering in from the west \nwill allow precipitation to mix with or change over to snow. \nAdditionally, strong north-to-south oriented frontogenesis is \nprogged to develop over east-central Illinois Wednesday night, \nwith dynamic cooling helping to lower temperatures and increase \nsnow likelihood. However, poor mid and upper level lapse rates \nand a lack of notable negative EPV provide mixed signals on \nmesoscale banding potential. Minor track differences between the\nGFS and ECMWF also add uncertainty regarding how far west the \nprecipitation shield will extend.\n\nRegarding temperatures, NBM guidance is undercutting non-bias- \ncorrected guidance, which may result in the statistical snow \noutput skewing high. Currently, NBM 25th percentile snowfall is \ntrace amounts up to around 2 inches, while the 75th percentile \njumps to 2 to 5 inches for most of central Illinois (with the \nexception of far east-central Illinois, where 75th percentile \namounts are 1 to 2 inches due to a later changeover to snow). \n0-2 inches of snow is probably a good starting point west of \nI-57, with lower confidence in those 2-5 inch totals if and \nwherever mesoscale banding is able to develop.\n\nModerating Temperatures (Friday Through the Weekend)...\nAs the upper low departs east from Friday through the weekend, \nupper level flow will gradually become zonal, allowing \ntemperatures to moderate back toward seasonal norms. With the \njet stream spreading across the Midwest and Great Lakes, \nadditional precipitation cannot be entirely ruled out, though \nblended NBM guidance keeps PoPs at or below 15 percent. As an \nexample, GFS and ECMWF both show a clipper-like system impacting\nthe state Saturday night, but timing and placement differences \nwash this signal out in the blended guidance.",
